<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bhopal State</h1><p class="st-meta">1707 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Bhopal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Afghan-founded Muslim state of central India, famous for its century of rule by four successive women, the Begums of Bhopal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan-of-bhopal">Dost Mohammad Khan</a>, an Afghan soldier who had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, took advantage of imperial decline after 1707 to seize lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. He fortified the old town of Bhopal and built the fort of Fatehgarh, establishing a Muslim dynasty over a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bhopal resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> pressure and allied with the British in 1818. From 1819 to 1926 four women ruled in turn: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudsia-begum">Qudsia Begum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikandar-begum">Sikandar Begum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan-begum">Shah Jahan Begum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-jahan-begum">Sultan Jahan Begum</a>, who built mosques, schools, railways and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-ul-masajid">Taj-ul-Masajid</a>. It was a nineteen-gun salute state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nawab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamidullah-khan">Hamidullah Khan</a> held out against accession until 1949, when Bhopal became a centrally administered state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 1956 it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bhopal-state.jpg" alt="Bhopal State" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Ricordisamoa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drapeau_Bhopal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1707 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bhopal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dost Mohammad Khan, Sikandar Begum, Hamidullah Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Urdu, Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bhopal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=bhopal-state" aria-label="Bhopal State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6746 1320 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bhopal State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7066 1576L6991 1757M7066 1576L7364 1601M7066 1576L7364 1602M7066 1576L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7066 1576h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7066 1576h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bhopal. <a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=bhopal-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
